NEAR-02: Jacobians by forward differences in ROS2 and Newton

ROS2 and the Newton equilibrium solver compute their Jacobians by central
differences, two evaluations per column. Use forward differences from the
values each already has at y: one evaluation per column, through one shared
helper with one step size. Newton keeps central differences for a single
equation. Results change at the level of rounding.

Requires: none
Results: near (concentrations move at the level of rounding; see 2-near-identical/README.md)

Apply from the root of an EPANET-MSX checkout at 85a303d, after FIX-01, FIX-02
and BIT-01 to BIT-17 (the other near-identical changes are not needed):
    git apply --whitespace=nowarn NEAR-02.patch
or use tools/build-msx.sh, which applies the series in order.

diff --git a/src/solver/msxutils.c b/src/solver/msxutils.c
index 85493a8..56dc6f6 100644
--- a/src/solver/msxutils.c
+++ b/src/solver/msxutils.c
@@ -538,3 +538,57 @@ int jacobian(double *x, int n, double *f, double *w, double **a,
 */
 
 }
+
+//=============================================================================
+
+int jacobianFwd(double *x, int n, double *f, double *w, double **a,
+                const char *unused,
+                void (*func)(double, double*, int, double*, void*), void* data)
+/*
+**  Purpose:
+**    computes Jacobian matrix of F(t,X) at given X by forward differences
+**
+**  Input:
+**    x[1..n] = vector of function variables
+**    n = number of variables
+**    f[1..n] = function values at x
+**    w[1..n] = a work vector
+**    unused[1..n] = 1 for each variable on which no function depends
+**                   (its column is 0, found without evaluating the
+**                   functions), or NULL
+**    func = user supplied routine that computes the function
+**           values at x
+**    data = pointer passed on to func.
+**
+**  Output:
+**    a[1..n][1..n] = coeffs. of the Jacobian matrix.
+**
+**  Returns:
+**    the number of times that func() was called.
+**
+**  Note:
+**    takes n evaluations of the functions where jacobian() takes 2n:
+**    column j is (F(x + eps e_j) - F(x)) / eps, with the step eps that
+**    jacobian() takes on either side of x (on one side only where x[j]
+**    is 0, which gives the same column as here).
+*/
+{
+    int    i, j, nfcn = 0;
+    double temp, eps = 1.0e-7;
+
+    for (j=1; j<=n; j++)
+    {
+        if ( unused && unused[j] )
+        {
+            for (i=1; i<=n; i++) a[i][j] = 0.0;
+            continue;
+        }
+        temp = x[j];
+        x[j] = temp + eps;
+        func(0.0, x, n, w, data);
+        nfcn++;
+        for (i=1; i<=n; i++) a[i][j] = (w[i] - f[i]) / eps;
+        x[j] = temp;
+    }
+    return nfcn;
+}
